(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609081500-kotoba-native-arena-and-linear-returning")
  (:adr/title "A Kotoba guest runs natively: exact artifact reads, a caller's oracle budget, named vector arenas, and an accumulator that may be handed back")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-08")
  (:adr/body "A Kotoba guest could not be a native binary for two reasons at once, and
neither was where the search started. This session found both by RUNNING
things rather than reading them, and closed both.

## 1. The compiler could not read its own artifacts exactly

`amu extract-native` read the sealed `.kexe` with `cljs.reader/read-string`,
which returns a `js/Number` for every integer token. A Number cannot hold an
i64. Measured on SHA-512 compiled to aarch64-macos: 347 integer literals, 106
past 2^53, the first round constant coming back 4794697086780616226 ->
4794697086780617000. The seal is over the exact values, so the command
refused its own compiler's output as \"artifact integrity mismatch\".

Nothing here could see it. Every fixture that reached the command had
constants under 2^53, where a Number is exact.

Reading exactly then exposed three more, each of which had been invisible for
the same reason -- nothing JVM-free had ever executed the path:

  - osaho: the native admission gate did not name the i64 shift family, so
    SHA-512 (rotations over a :vector-i64) was refused for aarch64 with a
    message about typed values that named no shift
  - kotoba-verifier: seven gates in the .cljs branch of verify-artifact! read
    the JVM's representation. The seventh was not a refusal but a host
    TypeError -- a param index out of an artifact is a bigint and nth will
    not take one -- reported as :kotoba/internal-error, which sends the reader
    to look at the compiler
  - kotoba-mir: aarch64-fuse-zero-equality-branches tested its constant with
    zero?, false for a bigint, so the fusion never fired for a literal that
    came from the READER -- every literal in real source. (if (= a 0) a a) was
    24 bytes on the JVM-free front and 12 through the JVM, FOR THE SAME
    PROGRAM. Emission had stopped being a function of the KIR value, which is
    what makes an artifact verifiable at all

## 2. lower's constant oracle ran on a budget no caller could name

`kotoba.kir/lower` seals a pure entry's value by EXECUTING it, on a private
100,000. `--fuel` reached the artifact's :limits and the verifier's
re-execution and stopped there, so a program costing more was refused as
`fuel-exhausted` whatever its author declared. Measured on the X25519
Montgomery ladder: identical refusals at --fuel 60000000 and at
--fuel 9007199254740991, the largest budget KIR admits.

## 3. The loader's vector arenas were compile-time constants with no name

kexe_loader.c bounded vector allocation twice over -- a table of handles and
an arena of items -- and its own comment recorded that raising either \"is a
separate decision with its own fail-closed argument, and it has not been
made\".

The decision made here is NOT a bigger constant. Both numbers stay where they
were and become DEFAULTS of a per-run budget (KEXE_VECTOR_CAPACITY /
KEXE_VECTOR_ITEM_CAPACITY), parsed the way KEXE_FUEL was, capped so the two
arenas together cannot exceed 1 GiB. A guest that ran before runs
identically; one that did not still refuses unless its caller asks.

The half worth more than the raise: exhaustion had no NAME. All ten arena
sites raised SIGILL and the report described the PAIR heap, untouched by
vector work, so a guest that outgrew an arena looked like a miscompilation.
Both loaders now name which arena filled and carry both in every report.

## 4. Why 32 million items, and the language change that answered it

X25519 took 1,165,132 handles and 31,933,313 items -- 274 MiB. Not a large
working set; one small working set copied several hundred thousand times.

The mechanism, measured with four probes: A LINEAR VECTOR MAY NOT BE
RETURNED. position-ok? treats a bare target as an escape, correctly, so a
function that builds a vector and hands it back can never write in place. The
identical fused read-modify-write loop is refused when its base case returns
the handle and admitted when the base case consumes it.

kotoba-sema a2ee65ac answers it over the MODULE: module-linear-returning is a
greatest fixpoint -- assume every parameter qualifies, drop those whose body
or whose call sites refuse them, repeat. Sound by induction on call depth.
TWO obligations, neither sound alone, each tested alone: the callee lets the
handle escape nowhere but its return, and every caller gives it up at the
call.

Result, same programs and same answers, on aarch64 under kexe_loader:

    field self-check   2,012 handles / 46,176 items -> 1,244 / 23,648
    X25519 ladder  1,165,132 / 31,933,313          -> 300,908 / 6,366,337

274 MiB down to about 54.

## What is executed, and where

  - SHA-512 (org-nist-sha2) in the Kotoba guest subset: compiles to
    aarch64-macos, extracts, and the loader answers 0 -- and 1 with one
    expected word perturbed
  - x25519.field: 0 on the KIR oracle, 0 on wasm32, 0 as a native aarch64
    binary; 1 with one expected byte perturbed
  - x25519.core (the ladder): 0 on the KIR oracle and 0 as a native aarch64
    binary against RFC 7748 section 5.2 vector 1, under a raised arena; 1
    with one expected byte perturbed

## Two defects found in work landed EARLIER TODAY by this same session

Both are recorded because the pattern matters more than either.

  - kotoba-sema deccd54 put a bare (catch :default ...) in a .cljc. Clojure
    reads :default as a class name and refuses to compile the file, so the
    repository's whole JVM suite stopped running -- and it went in green
    because the run that reported green never compiled that namespace on the
    JVM. Fixed in 92010a51: the guard is :cljs only, because a Clojure set
    hashes a BigInt without complaint and a JVM catch there could only
    swallow a real error
  - the kotoba/x25519 guest, landed the previous day, had FIVE defects and no
    entry to run. pack returned thirty-two zeros for every input; mul dropped
    limbs 16..30 of every product; carry never stripped limb 15; the ladder
    took its bits from the u-coordinate field element rather than the clamped
    scalar; final-x inverted the unswapped lane. It compiled, and compiling
    was read as working

## Known hole, left open deliberately and asserted as such

A function whose vector PARAMETER is linear in its own body earns
vector-assoc! whatever its callers do, and has since the gate existed. It is
observable: a bump helper called from a main that then reads its own handle
answers 2 as an aarch64 binary and should answer 1 -- a reader saw a store it
never asked for, and main writes no bang so the gate never looks at it.

module-linear-returning is the machinery that would close it. REQUIRING it
refuses two shapes that work today: a public helper whose callers are in
another module (torihiki.book's slab/add!, which ADR-2609010500 exists for)
and any function this module does not see called. Whole-program linking
answers the first. That is a decision about the language, so the widening
landed and the hole is asserted as an admission in kotoba-sema's sema_test,
named so that closing it is visible, with a note to invert the assertion.

## Landed

  osaho 73243145      i64 shift family admitted beside a typed value
  osaho c24c92a1      lower takes :oracle-fuel; default unchanged
  kotoba-verifier ea50d8f2   seventh representation gate; fixture rebuilt
  kotoba-mir ac690136 selection depends on the value, not the host's spelling
  kotoba-native a5711bdc     that, pinned
  artifact 678c0095 / a0b36af3 / e827c9ef   reviewed loader identities
  kototama-native f6adc42f   the report carries the two arenas
  kotoba-sema 92010a51       the JVM suite runs again
  kotoba-sema a2ee65ac       linear-returning
  kotoba-lang 49988eb7 / fc25c7ee / bb072c76   the decision and its evidence
  org-ietf-x25519 8bee1104 / 85555d48   the guest, corrected and in place
  amu 473e84ab (#888)  extract-native reads i64 exactly
  amu 6e1c81db (#889)  the oracle runs on the declared budget

## Resume point

  1. amu PR 891 (agent/native-vector-arena) -- the loader arenas. Green when
     last measured; merge it
  2. carry is the remaining arena cost in x25519.field, and the reason is
     precise: it reads limb i, computes a carry from it, and writes limbs i
     and i+1. Hoisting the read into a let makes it a second use. Admitting a
     let-bound read of v at k whose only other mention is a write at k in the
     let's body is the same soundness argument with the binding in between
  3. Ed25519 needs the Edwards layer and pow2523 on top of the corrected
     field. Verify is two scalar multiplications, so budget roughly twice the
     ladder: about 600k handles and 13M items after the in-place work
  4. The known hole above, if the language decides for whole-program linking")))
